Version history:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

0.93.1 (22-Sep-2026)
  * Shared-drive robustness fixes for real-world servers (chiefly macOS /
    NuoRDS). Following the 0.93 release, several cases that only arise against
    live servers were corrected: renaming a file while its handle is still
    open; enumerating extensionless files (now reported with a .dat
    extension); delete-on-close of temporary files held by more than one
    handle; real directory creation; volume-information queries (free space
    and filesystem attributes); name clashes between an "X/zip" file and an
    "X" directory; and case-insensitive / Unicode volume flags. Copying files
    on and off the shared drive, and unzipping onto it with a suitable tool,
    are all more reliable as a result.
  * The RDPDiskLog diagnostic trace is now written only when verbose logging
    is enabled (the -v option or RDPClient$Debug), so normal use no longer
    leaves a log file on the shared drive.
  * macOS note (NuoRDS servers). The Mac's built-in Archive Utility cannot
    expand a .zip archive directly onto the shared drive: it reports "Error
    45" or "unsupported format" and does nothing. This is a macOS limitation,
    not a fault in the shared drive -- macOS presents the redirected drive as
    a zero-capacity network volume, and Archive Utility refuses to stage an
    extraction there. Copying files on and off the shared drive works
    normally. To unzip onto the shared drive, use a third-party tool such as
    Keka (or 'unzip' / 'ditto' in Terminal), or expand the archive to a local
    Mac folder and copy the results across.

0.93 (21-Sep-2026)
  * Shared drive. A RISC OS directory can now be shared with the remote Windows
    session as a network drive (\\tsclient\RISCOS), for moving files both ways.
    Browse it in Windows Explorer; files copy off and on, open, rename and
    delete. RISC OS filetypes map to Windows extensions (via the system MimeMap
    module, with a built-in fallback table), spaces in names are preserved, and
    correct file dates are shown on both sides. Files stored on RISC OS with the
    extension in their name (e.g. "MyFile/pdf") open correctly from Windows too.
    Enable it with '-r disk:NAME=<RISC OS path>', or set it in the connection
    editor.
  * The connection editor gains a 'Shared folder' field: drag a folder from the
    Filer onto the editor to share it, and it is saved with the connection.

0.92.2 (19-Sep-2026)
  * Security hardening. Saved-connection input is validated and sanitised: the
    connection Name is restricted to safe characters, and field values are
    stripped of control characters and quotes, so a crafted connection cannot
    write outside its directory or inject a command into its launch Obey. Two
    server-controlled length reads in the core RDP / certificate parsing are
    now bounds-checked.
  * The display-mode entries (Window, Full window, Full screen) are grouped
    under a single 'Display' submenu on the iconbar menu.
  * Licence: a long-standing GPL compliance correction. The code has been GPL
    v3 since the original 2010 port (inherited from rdesktop 1.6.0); every
    earlier package bundled the wrong GPL v2 licence text by mistake. Corrected
    here -- the shipped licence file only, not a relicensing.

0.92.1 (16-Sep-2026)
  * Connects to more servers. A server configured for Standard (legacy) RDP
    Security only refuses the TLS offer (RDP negotiation failure code 2,
    'SSL not allowed by server'); instead of failing, the client now retries
    automatically with Standard security and connects.
  * Added a 'Standard security only (no TLS)' option -- the -S flag, and a
    checkbox in the connection editor -- to force Standard security (e.g. to
    probe an old server, or on a trusted LAN).
  * The RDP negotiation trace is now shown by the -v option / RDPClient$Debug
    (previously it was only in debug builds), to help diagnose connection
    problems. The Standard-security fallback only happens when the server
    explicitly refuses TLS, never on a failed TLS handshake.

0.92.0 (16-Sep-2026)
  * Added a connection manager. The iconbar 'Connections' menu now lists your
    saved connections, with 'New connection' at the top; each can be connected
    to, edited or deleted (delete asks first). A new editor sets the server,
    port, user, password, domain, screen size, colours, display mode and
    speed, so you no longer need to hand-write a connection Obey file. Saved
    connections are stored as Obey files in Choices:RDPClient.Connections.
  * Resolution and colours can be set to "Same as RISC OS" to match the
    current screen mode.
  * Added a password field, masked on screen. Note the password is saved as
    plain text in the connection file, as RDP command-line login requires.
  * Fixed full-screen mode: a saved connection set to full screen now honours
    it (the display mode was not being sent correctly before).
  * The startup banner now credits both the original author (Andrew Sellors)
    and the 32-bit update (Andrew Youll), and lists the -v trace option.

0.91.1 (16-Sep-2026)
  * Fixed connecting to Microsoft Windows servers over TLS. Modern Windows
    enforces two Enhanced RDP Security requirements the client was not meeting
    and dropped the connection during setup. The decisive one is an anti-man-
    in-the-middle check: the client must echo back the security protocol the
    server selected during negotiation, so a middle-man cannot silently
    downgrade the security. The client now sends this, and also advertises no
    RDP-layer encryption under TLS, as required. Non-Windows TLS servers (xrdp,
    NuoRDS) were never affected.
  * TLS errors now report the underlying AcornSSL message instead of a bare
    error number.
  * Added a protocol trace for diagnosing connection problems: off by default,
    enabled with the -v command-line option or by setting the system variable
    RDPClient$Debug (e.g. "Set RDPClient$Debug 1" in a connection Obey file).

0.91 (15-Sep-2026)
 * Added RDP-over-TLS support via the RISC OS AcornSSL module. When AcornSSL is
   present the client offers TLS in the RDP negotiation and upgrades the
   connection to TLS if the server selects it, so it can connect to servers
   that require TLS (modern Windows, TLS-configured xrdp / NuoRDS). Servers
   that only do standard RDP security connect as before; without AcornSSL the
   client is unchanged.
 * The Status window shows "TLS" in the encryption field during a TLS session.
 * Note: this is TLS encryption only, not TLS/NLA - NLA/CredSSP is not
   supported (a server that requires NLA is refused). When the server
   presents its certificate, AcornSSL shows a dialogue to accept or reject
   it, so a self-signed certificate can be accepted interactively.
 * Fixed a null-pointer crash in the display poller that could abort the
   client when a connection was dropped or re-established.

0.90.3 (14-Sep-2026)
 * Pasting text from a RISC OS application into the remote session now works
   (the RISC OS -> server clipboard direction). The client offers both CF_TEXT
   and CF_UNICODETEXT and, when the server requests Unicode (xrdp, Windows,
   NuoRDS), converts the RISC OS text to UTF-16LE with a single NUL terminator.
   An earlier double terminator was accepted by xrdp but rejected by
   NuoRDS/macOS.
 * Clipboard data offered by a RISC OS app in any filetype is now accepted,
   asking the app to supply text on save, so word-processor sources (e.g.
   Writer+) are handled where they can export plain text.
 * Re-enabled the -Otime speed optimisation (removed in 0.90.1); it is safe
   now that -za1 forces aligned code generation.

0.90.2 (14-Sep-2026)
 * Resolved the entire "type 20" CPU-alignment abort class - including 8-bit
   (256-colour) mode - by compiling with -za1 (disable unaligned loads/stores),
   the Norcroft 5.18 equivalent of the old -memaccess. 24-bit and 8-bit both
   work with CPU alignment checking left ON.
 * Fixed a NULL-username crash / corrupt login field when connecting with no
   username set (seen with xrdp).
 * Fixed the black square around standard-size mouse pointers (a pointer-mask
   precedence bug). Very large / HD cursors from some Linux desktops may still
   be imperfect.
 * Fixed pasting text from the remote session into RISC OS (the server ->
   RISC OS clipboard direction: ownership handling, CF_UNICODETEXT negotiation
   and UTF-16 -> Latin-1 conversion).

0.90.1 (12-Sep-2026)
 * Fixed "type 20" data aborts that occurred with CPU alignment checking
   turned on (the default on ARMv7 machines such as the Raspberry Pi 4). The
   online build compiler (Norcroft 5.18) does not support the -memaccess flag
   the original binary used, so some 16-bit values were being read with
   unaligned word loads; these have been corrected in the source. 24-bit
   display now connects with alignment left ON.
 * Removed the -Otime optimisation (it encouraged the unaligned accesses
   above).
 * Restored crash/postmortem capture to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.RDPClient.Error to
   aid bug reports.
 * Enlarged the 8-bit pixel translation buffer to its correct size (it had
   overrun a fixed 256-byte buffer).
 * Note: 8-bit (256-colour) mode still has outstanding issues and is being
   addressed for 0.90.2; 24-bit is the recommended mode.

0.90 (09-Sep-2026)
 * Added mouse scroll wheel support: wheel movements over the RDP display
   (in windowed, fullwindow and fullscreen modes) are now sent to the server.
 * Rebuilt as a 32-bit application for RISC OS 5.

